{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.Irreducibility","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.Irreducibility","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.HoefsmitRep","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormal21_no_common_eigenvector","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.seminormal21_no_common_eigenvector","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/Irreducibility.lean#L51-L98","doc":"**The seminormal `(2,1)` representation is irreducible** (`q ≠ 0`):\n`S0` and `S1` have no common eigenvector, hence no common `1`-dimensional\ninvariant subspace, hence the `2`-dimensional representation is irreducible. ","signatureChunk":0}]}